Let's take a closer look at the `python` module. In this case, there
are four different versions: `Python-bundle-PyPI/2023.06-GCCcore-12.3.0`, 
`Python/2.7.18-GCCcore-12.3.0`, `Python/2.7.18-GCCcore-12.3.0` and the version 
from `Anaconda3/2024.02-1`.
How do we load each copy and which copy is the default?

In this case, `Python/3.11.3-GCCcore-12.3.0` has a `(d)` next to it. This indicates that it
is the default - if we type `module load Python`, this is the copy that will be
loaded.

```
{{ site.remote.prompt }} module load Python
{{ site.remote.prompt }} python --version
```
{: .language-bash}

```
Python 3.11.3
```
{: .output}

So how do we load the non-default copy of a software package? In this case, the
only change we need to make is be more specific about the module we are
loading. There are four Python modules: `Python-bundle-PyPI/2023.06-GCCcore-12.3.0`, 
`Python/2.7.18-GCCcore-12.3.0`, `Python/2.7.18-GCCcore-12.3.0` and the version 
from `Anaconda3/2024.02-1`.
To load a non-default module, we need to make add the version number after the
`/` in our `module load` command

```
{{ site.remote.prompt }} module load Python/2.7.18-GCCcore-12.3.0
```
{: .language-bash}

```
The following have been reloaded with a version change:
  1) Python/3.11.3-GCCcore-12.3.0 => Python/2.7.18-GCCcore-12.3.0
```
{: .output}

What happened? The module command is telling us that we cannot have two `Python`
modules loaded at the same time as this could cause confusion about which
version you are using. It has removed version 3.11.3 and repalced it with 2.7.18.

```
{{ site.remote.prompt }} python --version
```
{: .language-bash}

```
Python 2.7.18
```
{: .output}

We now have successfully switched from Python 3.11.3 to 2.7.18.

As switching between different versions of the same module is often used you
can use `module swap` rather than `module load`. 
The equivalent of the steps above would be:

```
{{ site.remote.prompt }} module unload Python/2.7.18-GCCcore-12.3.0
{{ site.remote.prompt }} module load Python
Python 3.11.3
{{ site.remote.prompt }} python --version
{{ site.remote.prompt }} module swap Python Python/2.7.18-GCCcore-12.3.0

The following have been reloaded with a version change:
  1) Python/3.11.3-GCCcore-12.3.0 => Python/2.7.18-GCCcore-12.3.0

{{ site.remote.prompt }}  python --version
Python 2.7.18
```
{: .language-bash}

This achieves the same result as unload followed by load but in a single step.
